WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.210
Like many adaptations, Netflix's Bird Box
underwent a number of changes on its journey

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:09.560
from the page to the screen, and some of them
are pretty surprising. Loosen your blindfold

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.160
and take a look at some of the biggest differences
between the book and film versions of Bird

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:14.660
Box.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.640
The Bird Box book takes a slow-burn approach
to the apocalypse, with the spread of the

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:24.860
monsters unfolding over a span of months.
The movie speeds things up dramatically, compressing

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.860
much of the chaos into a single afternoon.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.880
In the book, Malorie doesn't join up with
other survivors until three months after America

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:37.069
starts seeing a wave of mysterious deaths.
Instead, she and her sister Shannon — whose

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:41.160
name was changed to Jessica for the movie
— first take shelter together in a rented

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.019
home they share. The sisters board up the
house, watching the news and listening to

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:46.900
the radio for updates on the outside world.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.879
At first, Malorie's not even sure if the monsters
are real, only becoming convinced when they

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:56.850
inspire her sister to take her own life. In
the movie, Jessica sees one of the monsters

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.380
as she's driving, causing her to crash the
car and walk into traffic before Malorie's

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:01.880
eyes.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.900
In the book, Shannon dies more quietly inside
the sisters' house, when a window becomes

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:12.350
exposed while Malorie's in another room. Whatever
Shannon sees inspires her to fatally stab

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.780
herself with a pair of scissors. It's only
at this point that Malorie becomes convinced

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.960
the monsters are real, after which she finally
decides to leave and seek out the help of

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:21.460
others.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.800
In the movie, Malorie finds refuge in a house
with a group of strangers, with whom she hides

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:31.010
out for a couple of weeks. In the book, though,
she sticks with this group for roughly half

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.520
a year — and it's a pretty different group,
too.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.931
Many of the book's survivors were replaced
with new creations for the movie. These added

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:42.620
characters include Charlie, the supermarket
employee and aspiring novelist; Greg, the

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:47.520
owner of the house; and Lucy, a young woman
who teams up with Felix to steal the group's

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.020
only car.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.830
John Malkovich's Douglas shares a few traits
with a character from the book named Don,

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:57.581
with both men being blunt, cynical, and concerned
about the group's limited resources. He's

00:01:57.581 --> 00:02:01.200
one of the movie's most entertaining elements,
and for the most part, he's made up.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.000
"What are they gonna do at Greg's, starve?"

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.640
"Better them than us."

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:12.380
Both versions of Bird Box feature the characters
Tom and Gary, with Gary's role as an agent

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:17.410
of the monsters staying more-or-less the same.
But Tom, played by Trevante Rhodes, has a

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.900
much bigger role in the movie than he does
in the book.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:25.500
"I'm not leaving you. I'm not leaving you."
"Listen to me — listen to me. I love you

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.440
so much. I love you. Go."

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:35.580
In the novel, Tom is a blue-eyed, sandy-haired
man who dies when Gary betrays the survivors,

00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:40.099
never becoming romantically involved with
Malorie. In the movie, Tom manages to kill

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:45.129
Gary, keeping him from hurting Malorie and
the newborn kids. Afterwards, he spends five

00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:49.650
years living with Malorie in another location,
acting as a husband to her and a father to

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.940
the two children before sacrificing himself
to protect them all.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.280
Bird Box's creatures are a threat in both
versions of the story, but the movie makes

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.900
the monsters much more malicious. In the film,
there's no doubt that the monsters are trying

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.880
to hurt people, while the book is a little
bit more ambiguous.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:10.580
One notable example occurs during the river
journey, when Malorie and the kids run into

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:15.139
a monster. In the book, the creature attempts
to take off Malorie's blindfold, but actually

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.140
backs off when Malorie protests.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.920
It's implied in the book that the monsters
aren't necessarily evil — they're more of

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.300
a force of nature that neither the characters
nor the reader is able to totally understand.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.930
The movie monsters act more traditionally
villainous, attempting to manipulate people

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.680
with psychological tricks, like faking human
voices.

00:03:32.969 --> 00:03:37.439
Despite these differences, neither the book
nor the movie actually show what the monsters

00:03:37.439 --> 00:03:41.709
look like, which in the movie's case is a
pretty good thing, considering the silly design

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:42.900
they almost went with.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.320
In both versions of Bird Box, birds serve
a purpose as warning signs for people, like

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.060
canaries in a coal mine, indicating that danger
is close. In the movie, the birds are helpfully

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.549
immune to the monsters — but in the book,
animals are as vulnerable to the creatures

00:03:57.549 --> 00:04:02.629
as people are, making them less useful for
survival. The movie presents Malorie's birds

00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:06.939
as an essential asset for the river journey,
but in the book, the birds die before she

00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:11.689
even starts the trip. In one sequence during
the voyage, an entire flock of birds falls

00:04:11.689 --> 00:04:16.420
victim to the monsters, causing bird bodies
and blood to rain down around the rowboat.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:20.860
For a while in the book, Malorie also keeps
a border collie named Victor with her as a

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:25.770
seeing-eye dog. It's a clever arrangement
that ends in tragedy — when Victor is exposed

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:30.020
to one of the monsters, he reacts by fatally
chewing off his own legs.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.280
One of the most intense points in the movie
comes when Malorie and the kids encounter

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.400
rapids on the river. Knowing that her death
would doom the children, Malorie tells the

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.540
kids that one of them will have to take off
their blindfold and act as her navigator.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.800
Before her change of heart, it's heavily implied
that Malorie is going to choose Girl to make

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.260
the sacrifice, potentially dooming Olympia's
daughter while sparing her biological child.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.080
"Somebody has to look."
"I'll look."

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.860
"No, I will decide. I will decide, just give
me a s… Just give me a second."

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:02.300
While it's one of the most suspenseful parts
of the movie, this moral dilemma is completely

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:06.460
avoided in the book, where Malorie asserts
that she'll be the one to take off her blindfold.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.400
Don't be fooled, though — the book version
of Malorie is still a pretty intense parent.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.640
Both versions of Bird Box end with Malorie
and her kids reaching a safe haven, but the

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:20.010
lead-up to their journey has big differences.
In the movie, Malorie and Tom learn of the

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:24.690
potential refuge from a mysterious voice on
the radio. Tom is optimistic about the new

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:29.500
information, but Malorie is skeptical. It's
only after Tom's death that Malorie is pushed

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.400
into making the journey.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.310
In the book, Malorie knows about the sanctuary
for years, and raises her children with the

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:39.960
goal of eventually seeking it out. She vigorously
trains the children for the journey from a

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.420
very young age, teaching them to keep their
eyes closed and identify sounds, to the point

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:46.640
that their senses of hearing surpass hers.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:51.500
Sometimes her training takes an uncomfortably
brutal approach, with Malorie punishing the

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:55.720
children for opening their eyes by hitting
them with flyswatters. When they're babies,

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.960
she even briefly considers blinding them with
paint thinner. So if you thought movie Malorie

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:03.460
was hard to sympathize with, just know that
it could've been so much worse.

